    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1065">

                <pb facs="0907" n="791" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg791"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/3616.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Ambassador to
                        the Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegrams.—Paraphrases.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-04-16T21:00:00-06:36">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 16, 1912—9 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>At his own request I sent to the Minister for Foreign Affairs my note based on
                    Department’s April 14, 3 p.m., late in the evening, as the Minister wished to
                    avoid public expression. Comments on the alleged undiplomatic character of the
                    note and its publication appear in the press, with report that the Minister will
                    not accept the conclusions of the note. The Cabinet is now considering the
                    Government’s reply.</p>
                <p>The arms and ammunition for the American colony are now in the hands of the
                    committee.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
